<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toulouse</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toulouse is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As Tolosa it was the chief town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a> Tectosages and then a Roman city; it was the capital of the Visigothic kingdom from 418 to 507 and later of the counts of Toulouse, rulers of much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>, until the Albigensian Crusade brought it under the French crown in 1271. It is now a centre of the aerospace industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toulouse.jpg" alt="Toulouse" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frédéric Neupont · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toulouse_-_vue_du_Vieux_Toulouse_depuis_St_Sernin_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Simon de Montfort, leader of the Albigensian Crusade, was killed in 1218 by a stone from a siege engine outside <b>Toulouse</b>; an Occitan chronicle says women of the city worked the machine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the death of his nephew Acfred in 927 the Auvergnat line died out, and the ducal title was disputed between the counts of <b>Toulouse</b> and the counts of Poitou.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They were not always effective in pitched battle, losing at <b>Toulouse</b> in 439, barely winning at the Utus in 447, probably losing or drawing at the Catalaunian Plains in 451 and losing at Nedao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugh's real power was modest: the royal domain was a narrow strip around Paris and Orléans, while the dukes of Normandy, Aquitaine and Burgundy and the counts of Flanders, Champagne and <b>Toulouse</b> owed only loose homage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Alaric they sacked Rome in 410, carrying off Galla Placidia, the sister of the emperor Honorius; his successor Athaulf (410–415) took Narbonne and <b>Toulouse</b> in 413 and married Placidia, after which Honorius enlisted the Visigoths against the Vandals, Alans and Suebi in Hispania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Concerning Toleration of 1689 argued that the state had no business forcing belief, and Voltaire’s Treatise on Tolerance of 1763 was written after the Protestant merchant Jean Calas was executed in <b>Toulouse</b> in 1762 on a false charge of murdering his son to stop him becoming a Catholic.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li></ul></section>
